            "accept": {
              "amass": [
                "acquire"
              ],
              "acquire": [
                "secure"
              ]
            },
            "paragraphs": [
              "In my family, pictures were things to look at and talk about. They did not have to be expensive. That was an important part of my {{upbringing}}. My aunt often brought small prints to our house. Looking for work by local artists later became a favourite {{pursuit}} of mine.",
              "My aunt once asked what I would do if I were to {{inherit}} her pictures. I said I would keep them, but I knew very little about caring for old paper. After her death, the prints formed part of my {{inheritance}}. I began to read {{extensively}}, taking books home from the library and making notes about keeping pictures safe and dry.",
              "I also tried to {{secure}} a place on a short course at the local museum. A member of staff explained that {{conservation}} could begin with simple steps, such as keeping paper dry and out of strong sunlight. Drawings and letters went into blue and grey folders {{respectively}}, so that I could find them without opening every box.",
              "One shelf was kept {{exclusively}} for work by a local {{pioneer}} of colour printing. I did not try to {{amass}} piles of pictures just to make the collection bigger. Before agreeing to {{acquire}} another piece, I asked where it would go and whether I could care for it. Collecting should never mean living {{beyond one’s means}}.",
              "Eventually, I sent photographs of a few prints to the museum {{for consideration}}. The staff were planning a small display. Some prints were accepted {{on loan}}. They would return to me after the show, and {{ownership}} would remain with the family. That seemed a good way to share them without giving up the collection."
            ]
